MediaCodec: choose a video encoder that exists, and stop using one that cannot encode

and_vid_mediacodec.cpp picked its MediaCodec component from four hardcoded lists of OMX
component names, preferring the software one. Android 12 retired those names and Android
15 ships none of them -- but AMediaCodec_createCodecByName("OMX.google.h264.encoder")
still returns a handle, so codec_exists() accepted a component the platform cannot honour
and every dequeueInputBuffer on it failed for the life of the call. The camera captured,
the preview drew, and the stream carried 0 packets/s while the far end held its first
frame.

Measured handset-to-handset. A Galaxy M14 (Android 15) and a Pixel 10a (Android 17)
advertise only c2.* components and sent no video at all; a Galaxy M23 (Android 14), which
still has the OMX names, was unaffected -- which made it look like a network fault.

This patch:

  * enumerates every encoder the platform advertises for the MIME through Java's
    MediaCodecList (the NDK has no enumeration API before API 36) instead of guessing;

  * ranks them hardware-Codec2 first, then any other hardware, then Codec2 software,
    then the rest, using MediaCodecInfo.isHardwareAccelerated() where it exists;

  * replaces an encoder that will not configure, inside the same codec open, so the call
    that hits it still gets video (OMX.qcom.video.encoder.avc refuses every configure
    with -10000 on an SM-E236B);

  * condemns an encoder that configures and then starves -- AND_MEDIA_STARVED_LIMIT
    consecutive frames with no input buffer -- for the NEXT call;

  * keeps a last-resort pass: when every advertised encoder has been condemned it takes
    the best of them anyway, because being left with no candidate has no way back;

  * releases the encoder's output buffer on the three paths out of encode_begin that
    previously leaked it (getOutputBuffer failure, process_encode failure, and a payload
    too large for the caller's buffer). MediaCodec's output pool is finite and a leaked
    buffer is gone for the life of the component.

Starvation is deliberately NOT repaired on the live stream. Swapping the component under
a running call was tried and made matters worse: each replacement lasted a shorter time
than the last -- thirteen minutes, then forty-four seconds -- until every candidate was
condemned and the handset could encode nothing. The cause of that starvation is still
unknown; see the handoff notes.

The start-up probe deliberately does no more than create a component and delete it.
Configuring and starting a candidate there never returned on an SM-E236B when tearing
down an OMX.qcom encoder whose configure had just failed, leaving discovery stuck and the
account unregistered. A call that picks a poor encoder is a bad call; a stack that cannot
register is a dead phone.


